.\" aurora-lint - CERT C static analyzer
.\" Man page for aurora-lint(1)
.TH aurora-lint 1 "October 2026" "aurora-lint 0.6.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
aurora-lint \- CERT C compliance checker for C source code
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B aurora-lint
[\fIOPTIONS\fR] [\fIPATH\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B aurora-lint
is a static analysis tool that checks C source code
for violations of the SEI CERT C Coding Standard. It performs AST-based,
control-flow, dataflow, and inter-procedural analysis to detect defects
including null pointer dereferences, integer overflows, buffer overflows,
use-after-free, and resource leaks.
.PP
.B aurora-lint
requires no build system, no compilation and no
.IR compile_commands.json .
It parses C with tree-sitter rather than driving a compiler, so it needs
neither a toolchain, system headers, nor a working build, and will analyze
source that cannot currently be built. A
.IR compile_commands.json
and
.BR \-I / \-D
flags are used when supplied \(em they improve cross-file context and reduce
false positives \(em but are never required.
.PP
.B aurora-lint
analyzes C
.RI ( .c
and
.IR .h )
against the CERT C standard. It is not a C++ checker.
.PP
If \fIPATH\fR is omitted, the current directory is analyzed.
\fIPATH\fR may be a single C source file, a directory (analyzed recursively),
or a git repository (with \fB\-\-diff\fR to analyze only changed files).
.SH OPTIONS
.SS "Input & Configuration"
.TP
\fB\-m\fR, \fB\-\-manifest\fR \fIFILE\fR
Path to the rules manifest file (TOML format). The manifest controls which
rules are enabled and their severity levels. Default:
.IR rules_templates/rules\-all.toml .
.TP
\fB\-d\fR, \fB\-\-directories\fR \fIDIR\fR
Additional directories to pre-scan for function definitions, providing
cross-file context for inter-procedural analysis. May be specified multiple
times. For best results, include all source directories in the project.
.TP
\fB\-I\fR, \fB\-\-include\-path\fR \fIDIR\fR
Include search paths for resolving \fB#include\fR directives, analogous to the
compiler \fB\-I\fR flag. May be specified multiple times.
.TP
\fB\-\-compile\-commands\fR \fIFILE\fR
Read include search paths (\fB\-I\fR, \fB\-isystem\fR, \fB\-iquote\fR,
\fB\-idirafter\fR) and command-line macros (\fB\-D\fR, \fB\-U\fR) out of a
\fIcompile_commands.json\fR and feed them to the pre-scan. Optional: aurora-lint needs
no build system, and without this flag behavior is unchanged. No preprocessor is
run and the parse tree is untouched, so findings keep their source locations.
Paths in a database are absolute; if it was generated on another host, aurora-lint warns
rather than silently resolving nothing.
.TP
\fB\-\-system\-includes\fR
Also search the compiler's own built-in system header directories, discovered by
asking it (\fBcc \-E \-Wp,\-v \-\fR) and appended at lowest priority. A
compile database lists only the flags a build \fIpasses\fR, so it can never
contain these; with one, each compiler the database names is asked, and without
one the platform default \fBcc\fR is. Off by default because it spawns a
compiler. A compiler that cannot be run is reported and skipped, not fatal.
.TP
\fB\-\-report\-macro\-gaps\fR[=\fIJSON_FILE\fR]
After the scan, report where the macro-expansion engine was blind: macro
definitions it skipped (variadic, \fB#\fR/\fB##\fR, dead under the assumed
build configuration, ambiguous, or conflicting), \fB#include\fRs that
resolved to no file, and calls it could not expand or attribute. Prints a
per-kind summary to stdout after the findings;
\fB\-\-report\-macro\-gaps\fR=\fIFILE\fR (the \fB=\fR is required, so the path
is never mistaken for the scan target) also writes every row as JSON. Never
changes a finding \(em it is a parse-only second pass over the engine's own
decisions. See
.I docs/cli\-usage.rst
for the full list of \fBkind\fR values.
.TP
\fB\-\-suppress\-file\fR \fIFILE\fR
Path to a
.I suppress.toml
suppression file. If not specified, aurora-lint looks in the project root for
.IR suppress.toml ,
then
.I .aurora-lint\-suppress.toml
and
.IR .sqc\-suppress.toml .
Each entry is a \fB[[suppress]]\fR table, matched by hash (one line) or by
glob or prefix pattern (see \fBSuppression File\fR below).
.SS "Output & Reporting"
.TP
\fB\-e\fR, \fB\-\-export\fR \fIFILE\fR
Export violations to a file. The output format is determined by the file
extension:
.RS
.TP
.B .csv
Comma-separated values
.TP
.B .json
JSON array of violation objects
.TP
.B .xlsx
Excel workbook with formatted columns and severity coloring
.TP
.B .sarif\fR, \fB.sarif.json
SARIF v2.1.0 for IDE and CI/CD integration
.RE
.TP
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-verbose\fR
Increase output verbosity. May be specified multiple times:
.B \-v
shows per-rule progress;
.B \-vv
shows per-violation detail.
.SS "Analysis Modes"
.TP
\fB\-i\fR, \fB\-\-interactive\fR
Run in interactive terminal UI mode with keyboard navigation, filtering,
and inline code preview. Conflicts with \fB\-\-export\fR and
\fB\-\-generate\-suppression\fR. Requires a binary built with
\fB\-\-features tui\fR (disabled by default).
.TP
\fB\-\-diff\fR
Only analyze C files that are modified or new according to
.BR git\-diff (1).
Requires the target path to be inside a git repository.
.TP
\fB\-\-generate\-suppression\fR \fIFILE\fR:\fILINE\fR:\fIRULE\fR
Generate a suppression comment for the specified violation. Output is printed
to stdout. Conflicts with \fB\-\-interactive\fR and \fB\-\-export\fR.
.SS "Project-Relevance Detection"
.TP
\fB\-\-detect\-relevance\fR
Scan \fIPATH\fR and any \fB\-d\fR directories for evidence of threading
(\fBpthread\fR, \fI<threads.h>\fR, \fB_Atomic\fR) and Windows API usage, then
generate a rules manifest with the categorically-inapplicable rule classes
disabled (\fBCON\fR* if no threading evidence is found, \fBWIN\fR* if no
Windows API evidence is found). Does not run an analysis; pair with
\fB\-\-write\-manifest\fR to save the result. Detection is conservative: a
rule class is disabled only when no evidence of it is found anywhere in the
scanned corpus, including resolved \fB\-I\fR/\fB\-d\fR includes. An
unresolved include path leaves the affected rules enabled with an
\fB# unresolved: ...\fR comment rather than guessing.
.TP
\fB\-\-write\-manifest\fR \fIFILE\fR
With \fB\-\-detect\-relevance\fR, write the generated manifest to \fIFILE\fR.
Requires \fB\-\-detect\-relevance\fR.
.SS "Filtering"
.TP
\fB\-\-min\-severity\fR \fILEVEL\fR
Only report violations at or above this severity level.
\fILEVEL\fR is one of: \fBLow\fR, \fBMedium\fR, \fBHigh\fR, \fBCritical\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-rules\fR \fIRULE1\fR,\fIRULE2\fR,...
Only report violations from the specified rules. Comma-separated list of
CERT C rule identifiers (e.g., \fBARR30\-C,MEM31\-C,STR32\-C\fR).
.TP
\fB\-\-exclude\-all\fR \fIGLOB\fR
Leave files matching this path glob out of everything: nothing is reported
in them and the cross-file pre-scan does not read them. May be specified
multiple times. Useful for test harnesses, demo programs, build tooling and
checked-in amalgamations that do not link into the shipped product. Globs
are matched against each file's path relative to the scan root. A header in
a matching tree that a scanned file includes is still read through
.B \-I
include resolution. It changes what cross-file facts can hold, so it moves
the settings hash, and
.B \-\-load\-prescan
refuses a cache built under a different pre-scan scope.
.TP
\fB\-\-report\-exclude\fR \fIGLOB\fR
Report nothing in files matching this path glob, but still read them in the
cross-file pre-scan. May be specified multiple times. Useful for vendored
code the product links and ships: its definitions are the ones the product's
calls reach, but its findings are not the project's to fix. The project's own
.I toolchain.toml
.B [ignore].paths
are merged in with this meaning.
.TP
\fB\-\-prescan\-exclude\fR \fIGLOB\fR
Scan and report files matching this path glob, but leave them out of the
cross-file pre-scan. May be specified multiple times. Useful for stubs, fuzz
harnesses and alternate-platform files whose definitions would otherwise
count as another build of the functions the product calls. It moves the
settings hash, as
.B \-\-exclude\-all
does.
.TP
\fB\-\-exclude\fR \fIGLOB\fR
Deprecated: the same as
.BR \-\-report\-exclude ,
the meaning it has always had, and prints a warning on standard error. Use
.B \-\-report\-exclude
to keep that behaviour, or
.B \-\-exclude\-all
to leave the files out of the cross-file pre-scan too.
.PP
.BR \-\-exclude\-all ,
.B \-\-report\-exclude
and
.B \-\-exclude
are the only flags that remove files from the report. Unlike them,
.B \-d
only adds cross-file pre-scan context and never restricts which files are
analyzed. A manifest's
.B [scope]
table
.RB ( exclude_all ,
.BR report_exclude ,
.BR prescan_exclude )
adds to the command line's lists.
.SS "Exit Code Control"
.TP
\fB\-\-fail\-on\-violation\fR
Exit with code 1 if any violations are found.
.TP
\fB\-\-fail\-on\-severity\fR \fILEVEL\fR
Exit with code 1 if any violation meets or exceeds the specified severity.
\fILEVEL\fR is one of: \fBLow\fR, \fBMedium\fR, \fBHigh\fR, \fBCritical\fR.
.SS "Performance & Parallelism"
.TP
\fB\-j\fR, \fB\-\-jobs\fR \fIN\fR
Number of parallel analysis threads. \fB0\fR (default) auto-detects the
number of CPU cores. \fB1\fR forces sequential single-threaded analysis.
Each thread gets its own parser and rule registry; cross-file context is
shared read-only via \fB\-d\fR prescan data.
.TP
\fB\-\-save\-prescan\fR \fIFILE\fR
Save pre-scan context (function summaries, macro aliases, type information)
to a binary cache file. Useful for CI/CD workflows where prescan data can
be computed once and reused across runs.
.TP
\fB\-\-load\-prescan\fR \fIFILE\fR
Load pre-scan context from a cache file instead of scanning \fB\-d\fR
directories. The cache must have been generated by a prior
\fB\-\-save\-prescan\fR invocation.
.SS "Metadata"
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help message and exit.
.TP
\fB\-V\fR, \fB\-\-version\fR
Print version information and exit.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success. No violations found, or no violations meeting the failure threshold.
.TP
.B 1
Violations found and \fB\-\-fail\-on\-violation\fR or \fB\-\-fail\-on\-severity\fR
was set.
.TP
.B 2
Analysis error (invalid path, bad manifest, invalid suppression file, parse failure).
.SH CONFIGURATION
.SS "Rules Manifest"
The rules manifest is a TOML file that controls which rules are enabled.
Each rule has a section with \fBenabled\fR and \fBseverity\fR fields:
.PP
.RS
.nf
[ARR30-C]
enabled = true
severity = "High"

[MEM31-C]
enabled = true
severity = "Critical"
.fi
.RE
.PP
A default manifest enabling all rules is provided at
.IR rules_templates/rules\-all.toml .
.SS "Suppression File"
The suppression file
.RI ( suppress.toml )
suppresses findings the project has reviewed. Each entry is a
\fB[[suppress]]\fR table with a \fBname\fR and a \fBtool\fR
(\fB"aurora-lint"\fR, or \fB"*"\fR for every tool). An entry with a
\fBhash\fR suppresses one line and lapses when the line changes; an entry
without one matches by \fBfile_glob\fR, \fBrule\fR, \fBrule_glob\fR and
\fBfunction_prefix\fR, all of which must match:
.PP
.RS
.nf
# Suppress one line (from --generate-suppression)
[[suppress]]
name = "cast-int31"
tool = "aurora-lint"
file = "src/codec.c"
rule = "INT31-C"
hash = "a1b2c3d4e5f67890"
justification = "Intentional cast"

# Suppress a rule for a directory
[[suppress]]
name = "third-party-dcl"
tool = "aurora-lint"
file_glob = "third_party/**"
rule_glob = "DCL*"
justification = "External code"
.fi
.RE
.PP
The suppression file is invalid, and the run exits with status 2, when it
holds a table under any other name, such as \fB[[suppression]]\fR or
\fB[[wildcard]]\fR, or an entry with no \fBname\fR, with a key it does not
take, with a \fBhash\fR but no \fBfile\fR and \fBrule\fR, or with no
\fBhash\fR and nothing to match on. The error names every problem in the
file at once. Entries are checked only when their \fBtool\fR is
\fB"aurora-lint"\fR or \fB"*"\fR.
.SH EXAMPLES
Analyze a project directory:
.PP
.RS
.nf
aurora-lint /path/to/project
.fi
.RE
.PP
Analyze with cross-file context and include paths:
.PP
.RS
.nf
aurora-lint -d src/ -d lib/ -I include/ src/
.fi
.RE
.PP
Export results as SARIF for CI integration:
.PP
.RS
.nf
aurora-lint --diff --min-severity Medium \\
    --fail-on-severity High \\
    --export results.sarif .
.fi
.RE
.PP
Interactive mode:
.PP
.RS
.nf
aurora-lint -i /path/to/project
.fi
.RE
.PP
Filter to specific rules:
.PP
.RS
.nf
aurora-lint --rules ARR30-C,MEM31-C,EXP34-C src/
.fi
.RE
.PP
Generate a suppression for a specific finding:
.PP
.RS
.nf
aurora-lint --generate-suppression src/main.c:42:ARR30-C .
.fi
.RE
.PP
Parallel analysis using 8 threads:
.PP
.RS
.nf
aurora-lint -j 8 -d src/ -d lib/ src/
.fi
.RE
.PP
Exclude vendored code and test harnesses from a scan:
.PP
.RS
.nf
aurora-lint . --exclude-all "tests/**" --report-exclude "vendor/**" --exclude-all "**/onelua.c"
.fi
.RE
.PP
Generate a relevance-gated manifest for a codebase with no tailored
manifest, then use it for the actual scan:
.PP
.RS
.nf
aurora-lint /path/to/project --detect-relevance --write-manifest gated-rules.toml
aurora-lint /path/to/project -m gated-rules.toml
.fi
.RE
.SH FILES
.TP
.I rules_templates/rules\-all.toml
Default rules manifest enabling all CERT C rules.
.TP
.I .aurora-lint\-suppress.toml
Project-level suppression file (auto-detected in project root).
